+5.2 Conversely, suppose the canonical path law is invariant under the one-step left shift, so $\mathbb P_X(\theta^{-1}B)=\mathbb P_X(B)$ for every $B\in\mathcal E^{\otimes\mathbb N_0}$. The shift is measurable: the inverse image of each generating cylinder $\{z:z_{n_j}\in A_j,\ 1\le j\le r\}$ is the cylinder $\{z:z_{n_j+1}\in A_j,\ 1\le j\le r\}$. Induction therefore gives $\mathbb P_X(\theta^{-m}B)=\mathbb P_X(B)$ for every $m\ge0$ and every measurable $B$. For $0\le n_1<\cdots<n_r$ and $A_1,\ldots,A_r\in\mathcal E$, apply this identity to $C=\{z:z_{n_j}\in A_j,\ 1\le j\le r\}$. Since $\theta^{-m}C=\{z:z_{m+n_j}\in A_j,\ 1\le j\le r\}$, [F6] identifies the two sides with the shifted and unshifted finite-dimensional probabilities on the rectangle $\prod_{j=1}^r A_j$. The two finite-dimensional laws consequently agree on all measurable rectangles; [F5] extends their equality to $\mathcal E^{\otimes r}$. This proves the stated finite-dimensional shift invariance from canonical path-law invariance. [F5, F6, given]
